<<~ ahu slot=`#/security-cold-read-git-c-home-joshu-lares-scratc`>>

! Security cold read: `git -C /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/seat-harness/wt show HEAD --stat` then read tools/seat-broker.mjs, tools/seat-walls.mjs, tools/tree-export.mjs, tools/commit-relay.mjs and the proofs at the end of tools/commit-relay.test.mjs. Write nothing.
The claim: the broker executes NO code from a lead's tree (hooks, filters, submodules, credential helpers, installs, build/test/editor config) and a lead cannot grant its own push or forge a check receipt. Adversary = a lead that controls the worktree, its .git/config and hooks, files, symlinks, and the lane root.
Hunt for real bypasses: any git invocation that reads lane config/attributes/hooks and can exec (including `worktree add`, `worktree remove`, `status`, `ls-tree`, `read-tree`, `push` env/HOME, `core.*`/`include`/`url.*.insteadOf`/`remote.origin.pushurl`/`core.sshCommand` set in the lane's own .git/config that -c may not override); the receipt scheme (key location, mac coverage, replay, TOCTOU between verify and commit-tree); the readSafe race; bwrap wall gaps (env, /proc, /dev, network, writable binds, the key file reachable from the seat); the install seat (.npmrc allowlist gaps, workspace/pnpm-workspace.yaml, package.json pnpm fields, .pnpmfile.cjs executing during install even with &#45;-ignore-scripts); relay option validation (go file, key, receipts, iso outside lane); and whether each proof test truly fails without the defense. Rank HIGH/MED/LOW with file:line. Final message under 400 words.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

